## Why This Is Asked
Allocates a beginner to a practical Azure data ingestion pattern using Event Hubs, Functions, and ADLS Gen2. Emphasizes idempotency, deduplication, retries, and tenant isolation, plus basic observability and validation.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Event Hubs and capture
- Azure Functions (Event Hub trigger)
- ADLS Gen2 as sink
- Idempotent processing and de-dup with eventId
- Managed identity and security
- Backoff retries and latency validation

## Code Example
```javascript
module.exports = async function(context, eventHubMessages){
  for (const m of eventHubMessages) {
    const payload = JSON.parse(m.body);
    // dedupe by payload.eventId
    // write to ADLS Gen2 as Parquet (pseudo)
    await writeParquetToADLS(payload);
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this for peak multi-tenant load?
- How would you ensure exactly-once processing given external sinks?
- How would you monitor latency and data-loss metrics effectively?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end Azure-native, multi-tenant data platform design under regulatory constraints with real-time processing, governance, security, disaster recovery, and cost discipline.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant policy enforcement
- EU data residency with bring-your-own-key (BYOK) encryption
- Real-time event processing using Durable Functions
- Data governance with Purview and token-based sharing mechanisms
- Disaster recovery to paired region with automated failover strategies
- Cost optimization through serverless analytics, auto-scaling, and resource quotas

## Code Example
```javascript
// Durable Function snippet: policy check and redaction (pseudo-code)
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenantId = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  const eventData = req.body;

  // Per-tenant policy validation
  const policy = await getTenantPolicy(tenantId);
  if (!validateEvent(eventData, policy)) {
    context.res = { status: 403, body: 'Policy violation' };
    return;
  }

  // PII redaction
  const redactedData = redactPII(eventData, policy.piiRules);

  // Store to ADLS and trigger downstream processing
  await storeToADLS(tenantId, redactedData);
  await triggerSynapseProcessing(tenantId, redactedData);
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable, privacy-preserving federated pipeline on Azure, balancing data residency, governance, and cost. It requires concrete components, data flow, and failure-mode reasoning.

## Key Concepts

- Federated learning and secure aggregation in Azure Confidential Computing
- Tenant isolation via Delta Lake partitions on ADLS Gen2
- Central model registry in Azure ML with versioning
- Purview RBAC and BYOK for governance and encryption
- Cross-region DR and autoscale cost controls
- Latency targets and failure-mode handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo orchestrator for federated updates
const globalModel = loadRegistry('federated/global/v1');
for (const tenant of tenants) {
  const upd = getTenantUpdate(tenant);
  const sealed = seal(upd);
  const agg = secureAggregate([sealed]);
  pushToRegistry(agg, 'federated/global/v1');
}
```

